From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 11:20:06 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] util/disk_cache: claim a temporary entry with O_EXCL, not a
 lock

disk_cache_write_item_to_disk opened <entry>.tmp, took LOCK_EX on it
(flock, or an F_SETLK write lock where flock is missing) and held that
lock until it had renamed the file into place and closed it, and
disk_cache_recompute_size took the same lock on a temporary file older
than an hour before it removed it. A writer holds its lock for as long
as its thread is in the middle of a write, and the disk cache's threads
run in processes the system may suspend at any moment. iOS ends a
suspended process that holds a file lock (exception 0xdead10cc), so a
shader cache write in flight at the wrong moment took the whole
application with it.

Create the temporary file with O_CREAT | O_EXCL instead. Whoever
creates it owns the write of the entry, and EEXIST means another thread
or process is writing it, so the job is skipped, which is the outcome
of a failed flock. Nothing takes a lock any more, and the exclusion
between processes is the same, because O_EXCL is atomic on the file
system as well. Every path after the claim already ends with the file
renamed or unlinked, so a writer that finishes leaves nothing behind.

A writer that dies leaves its file, and a lock no longer reveals that
it is dead; only age does. A temporary file older than an hour is
removed without being opened, by the size recomputation and by the next
writer of the same entry, which replaces it. The second is needed
because the first runs only when a cache is created with an invalid
index or an eviction finds nothing to remove, so without it a dead
writer would block its entry for much longer than an hour. A live
writer is done in a moment.

The test stops a writer at its rename, with the temporary file complete
and still open, and probes the file with flock and F_OFD_GETLK from a
fresh open file description. It also checks that a second writer
leaves the entry to the first without waiting for it, that concurrent
puts of one key from several instances leave one valid entry and a size
that matches the disk, and that stale temporary files go by age alone,
even while a lock is held on one.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
